# The classifier stage

`WeftClassifier` labels each line with its weft kind by mode. It is a Mealy machine: the state is the previous weft and whether a heading has been seen, the input is a pattern probe of the line, and the output weft also feeds the next state. The wefts it emits are partial — the leading marker filled, the weft `incomplete` — and the tokeniser settles the rest.

## The classifier service

The state opens before the first heading — the Document Preamble — and tracks whether a heading has been emitted, whether the line sits inside a frontmatter block, whether it sits inside a table-of-contents section, and whether it sits inside a fenced block. `classifyWefts` threads that state through the line-range stream with `Stream.mapAccum`, classifying each line and carrying the result forward. A `---` toggles the frontmatter flag: the first one opens the block, the next closes it. A `{TOC}` heading opens a contents section, and the next top-level `#` heading closes it — so the toc flag follows every heading, set when the heading bears `{TOC}` and cleared by any other. A fence in prose opens a quote. Inside it a person quotes a loom verbatim, so a `#`, `=>`, or `~` there reads as text, not as a mark. Only a fence that matches the opening one closes the quote: one at the same column, at least as long, with nothing after it. So the state keeps the open quote's `Fence`, not a flag, and a shorter run of backticks inside the quote stays text. A box is a third such region. A line of three colons opens it, a line of three colons alone closes it, and the body between the two stays inert, so a box never reads as loom structure. A box and a quote never open inside each other. The state keeps whether a box is open, because the same line of three colons alone opens a box outside one and closes the box inside one.

Fenced code keeps two more pieces of state. An arrow with nothing after it on its line leaves the classifier waiting for a fence, and blank lines keep it waiting. If the first line that is not blank opens a fence, that line opens fenced code, and the state holds its `Fence` until the line that closes it. While fenced code is open, every line is code that no heading can close, so a Markdown file or a commented script keeps its `#` lines. Any other line ends the wait, and the block is plain code.

=>

type ClassifierState = {
  readonly prev: Option.Option<LoomWeft>
  readonly seenHeading: boolean
  readonly inFrontmatter: boolean
  readonly inToc: boolean
  readonly quote: Option.Option<Fence>
  readonly inBox: boolean
  readonly fenced: Option.Option<Fence>
  readonly awaitingFence: boolean
}

const initialState: ClassifierState = {
  prev: Option.none(),
  seenHeading: false,
  inFrontmatter: false,
  inToc: false,
  quote: Option.none(),
  inBox: false,
  fenced: Option.none(),
  awaitingFence: false,
}

export class WeftClassifier extends Context.Service<WeftClassifier>()('WeftClassifier', {
  make: Effect.succeed({
    classifyWefts:
      (text: string) =>
      (source: Stream.Stream<LineRange>): Stream.Stream<LoomWeft> =>
        Stream.mapAccum(
          source,
          () => initialState,
          (state, range) => {
            const lineText = text.slice(range[0], range[1])
            const weft = boxed(probeWeft(lineText, range, state), state)
            const frontmatterFence = weft.type === 'FrontmatterWeft' && weft.fence !== undefined
            const next: ClassifierState = {
              prev: Option.some(weft),
              seenHeading: state.seenHeading || weft.type === 'HeadingWeft',
              inFrontmatter: state.inFrontmatter !== frontmatterFence,
              inToc: weft.type === 'HeadingWeft' ? isTocHeading(lineText) : state.inToc,
              quote: nextQuote(state.quote, state.inBox, weft),
              inBox: nextBox(state.inBox, weft),
              fenced: nextFenced(state.fenced, weft),
              awaitingFence: nextAwaiting(state.awaitingFence, weft),
            }
            return [next, [weft]]
          },
        ),
  }),
}) {
  static readonly layer = Layer.effect(this, this.make)
}

## Classifying one line

`probeWeft` is the decision table. A `---` on the first line opens a frontmatter block, and every line through the closing `---` is a frontmatter weft; outside that block the rest of the table takes over. A fence in prose opens a quote the same way. Every line inside it is verbatim until the fence closes, so a `#`, `=>`, or `~` there stays text — the quote shows a loom, it does not compose one. A box is inert in just the same way, so its body never composes. A quoted line is a preamble weft in the document preamble, where nothing else may appear, and a prose weft inside a section. Inside a table-of-contents section every line is a toc weft — a deeper `##`–`######` heading and a `1.` list item alike — so a part heading never opens a section and a chapter entry never reads as prose; only the next top-level `#` closes the section and reads as an ordinary heading again. A `#` line is a heading in any other mode, and opens a section in preamble mode. Before the first heading every line is a preamble weft, since no transition fires there. After it, a line of three or more dashes is a divider in any mode, since a divider ends code as well as prose, just as a heading does. After it, the previous weft's mode decides. In preamble, `=>` opens code and `~` opens prose. In code, `~` returns to prose and every other line stays code. In prose, `=>` re-enters code and every other line stays prose. A line of dashes elsewhere is ordinary content, classified by the current mode like any other line — there is no separator weft.

Fenced code outranks every rule after the quote and the box. Inside it, the line that closes the fence is a fence weft, and every other line is code: a `#` line, an arrow, a tilde, a line of dashes. A waiting arrow comes next, and a fence there opens fenced code. Both checks run before the heading rule, since a heading is exactly what fenced code exists to hold. A quote or a box still comes first, so fenced code quoted in prose stays prose.

=>

const probeWeft = (lineText: string, range: LineRange, state: ClassifierState): LoomWeft => {
  const line = Option.match(state.prev, {
    onNone: () => 1,
    onSome: (w) => w.position.end.line + 1,
  })

  if (Option.isNone(state.prev) && isFrontmatterFence(lineText))
    return makeFrontmatterFence(lineText, line, range)

  if (state.inFrontmatter) {
    if (isFrontmatterFence(lineText)) return makeFrontmatterFence(lineText, line, range)
    return makeFrontmatterField(lineText, line, range)
  }

  if (Option.isSome(state.quote) || state.inBox)
    return state.seenHeading
      ? makeProseWeft(lineText, line, range)
      : makePreambleWeft(lineText, line, range)

  if (Option.isSome(state.fenced))
    return closesFence(state.fenced.value)(lineText)
      ? makeFenceWeft(lineText, line, range, 'close')
      : makeCodeWeft(lineText, line, range)

  if (state.awaitingFence && Option.isSome(openingFence(lineText)))
    return makeFenceWeft(lineText, line, range, 'open')

  const probe = probeOf(lineText)

  if (probe.kind === 'heading') {
    if (state.inToc && headingLevel(probe.m) > 1) return makeTocWeft(lineText, line, range)
    return makeHeadingWeft(lineText, line, range, probe.m)
  }

  if (state.inToc) return makeTocWeft(lineText, line, range)

  if (!state.seenHeading) return makePreambleWeft(lineText, line, range)

  if (isDivider(lineText)) return makeDividerWeft(lineText, line, range)

  return pipe(
    Match.value(modeOf(state.prev)),
    Match.when('preamble', () =>
      pipe(
        Match.value(probe),
        Match.when({ kind: 'arrow' }, (p) => makeArrowWeft(lineText, line, range, p.m)),
        Match.when({ kind: 'tilde' }, (p) => makeTildeWeft(lineText, line, range, p.m)),
        Match.orElse(() => makePreambleWeft(lineText, line, range)),
      ),
    ),
    Match.when('code', () =>
      pipe(
        Match.value(probe),
        Match.when({ kind: 'tilde' }, (p) => makeTildeWeft(lineText, line, range, p.m)),
        Match.orElse(() => makeCodeWeft(lineText, line, range)),
      ),
    ),
    Match.when('prose', () =>
      pipe(
        Match.value(probe),
        Match.when({ kind: 'arrow' }, (p) => makeArrowWeft(lineText, line, range, p.m)),
        Match.orElse(() => makeProseWeft(lineText, line, range)),
      ),
    ),
    Match.exhaustive,
  )
}

## Line positions

`linePos` is a whole-line span; `span` is an arbitrary offset range on a line. Both stamp the line number the classifier tracks.

=>

const linePos = (line: number, range: LineRange): Position => ({
  start: { line, offset: range[0] },
  end: { line, offset: range[1] },
})

const span = (line: number, start: number, end: number): Position => ({
  start: { line, offset: start },
  end: { line, offset: end },
})

## Mode and probe

Two axes drive the table. `modeOf` derives the mode — preamble, code, or prose — from the previous weft's type; a frontmatter weft and a toc weft both read as preamble, since neither sits in a section body, and a divider reads as preamble, since the lines after it open its division as the lines after a heading open its section. `probeOf` recognises the line as a heading, arrow, or tilde marker, or plain text, using the same `Probe` regexes the token schemas carry. `isFrontmatterFence` tests one more marker: the `---` that opens or closes the frontmatter block. `isDivider` tests a line of dashes past it. `headingLevel` counts a heading's `#` marks, so the top-level heading that closes a contents section is told from a deeper one, and `isTocHeading` tests a heading line for a `{TOC}` specifier, the marker that opens that section. `nextQuote` follows a quote. It opens one on a prose weft whose line opens a fence, which `isProseWeft` identifies, so a fence inside code is left to that code's own language, and it closes the quote on the line `closesFence` accepts. `nextQuote` refuses to open a quote while a box is open, and the section "Marking a box" refuses to open a box while a quote is open, so a box and a quote never nest. `nextFenced` follows fenced code: an opening fence weft sets it from its line, and a closing one clears it. A fence weft that closes fenced code hands the next line to prose, so `modeOf` reads it as prose. `nextAwaiting` keeps the wait for a fence: an arrow with no code on its line starts it, a blank code line keeps it, and any other line ends it.

=>

type Mode = 'preamble' | 'code' | 'prose'

const modeOf = (prev: Option.Option<LoomWeft>): Mode =>
  Option.match(prev, {
    onNone: () => 'preamble' as const,
    onSome: (w) =>
      pipe(
        Match.value(w),
        Match.when({ type: 'HeadingWeft' }, () => 'preamble' as const),
        Match.when({ type: 'PreambleWeft' }, () => 'preamble' as const),
        Match.when({ type: 'ArrowWeft' }, () => 'code' as const),
        Match.when({ type: 'CodeWeft' }, () => 'code' as const),
        Match.when({ type: 'FenceWeft' }, () => 'prose' as const),
        Match.when({ type: 'TildeWeft' }, () => 'prose' as const),
        Match.when({ type: 'ProseWeft' }, () => 'prose' as const),
        Match.when({ type: 'FrontmatterWeft' }, () => 'preamble' as const),
        Match.when({ type: 'TocWeft' }, () => 'preamble' as const),
        Match.when({ type: 'DividerWeft' }, () => 'preamble' as const),
        Match.exhaustive,
      ),
  })

type Probe =
  | { readonly kind: 'heading'; readonly m: RegExpMatchArray }
  | { readonly kind: 'arrow'; readonly m: RegExpMatchArray }
  | { readonly kind: 'tilde'; readonly m: RegExpMatchArray }
  | { readonly kind: 'plain' }

const headingProbe = Option.getOrThrow(getProbe(HeadingStartTokenSchema))
const arrowProbe = Option.getOrThrow(getProbe(ArrowTokenSchema))
const tildeProbe = Option.getOrThrow(getProbe(TildeTokenSchema))
const frontmatterFenceProbe = Option.getOrThrow(getProbe(FrontmatterFenceTokenSchema))

const isFrontmatterFence = (lineText: string): boolean => frontmatterFenceProbe.test(lineText)

const dividerProbe = Option.getOrThrow(getProbe(DividerTokenSchema))

const isDivider = (lineText: string): boolean => dividerProbe.test(lineText)

const isProseWeft = (weft: LoomWeft): boolean =>
  weft.type === 'PreambleWeft' || weft.type === 'ProseWeft' || weft.type === 'TildeWeft'

const nextQuote = (
  quote: Option.Option<Fence>,
  inBox: boolean,
  weft: LoomWeft,
): Option.Option<Fence> =>
  Option.match(quote, {
    onSome: (fence) => (closesFence(fence)(weft.source) ? Option.none() : quote),
    onNone: () => (!inBox && isProseWeft(weft) ? openingFence(weft.source) : Option.none<Fence>()),
  })

const nextFenced = (fenced: Option.Option<Fence>, weft: LoomWeft): Option.Option<Fence> =>
  Match.value(weft).pipe(
    Match.when({ type: 'FenceWeft', side: 'open' }, (open) => openingFence(open.source)),
    Match.when({ type: 'FenceWeft', side: 'close' }, () => Option.none<Fence>()),
    Match.orElse(() => fenced),
  )

const nextAwaiting = (awaiting: boolean, weft: LoomWeft): boolean =>
  Match.value(weft).pipe(
    Match.when({ type: 'ArrowWeft' }, (arrow) => arrow.source.trim() === arrow.arrow.source),
    Match.when({ type: 'CodeWeft' }, (code) => awaiting && code.source.trim() === ''),
    Match.orElse(() => false),
  )

const headingLevel = (m: RegExpMatchArray): number => m[0].replace(/[^#]/g, '').length

const tocSpecifier = /\{TOC\}/i

const isTocHeading = (lineText: string): boolean => tocSpecifier.test(lineText)

const probeOf = (lineText: string): Probe => {
  const h = headingProbe.exec(lineText)
  if (h) return { kind: 'heading', m: h }
  const a = arrowProbe.exec(lineText)
  if (a) return { kind: 'arrow', m: a }
  const t = tildeProbe.exec(lineText)
  if (t) return { kind: 'tilde', m: t }
  return { kind: 'plain' }
}

## Marking a box

A line in prose that starts with a run of colons is a box line, and `boxed` gives its weft the line's `Box` token. Only a preamble weft or a prose weft can be a box line, so a run of colons in code stays code, and a run of colons inside a quote stays quoted. `boxLineOf` finds the run through the `BoxFence` probe and records it as the box's fence. It leaves the name and the braces after the run to the tokeniser.

`sideOf` decides what the line does, from the length of the run and whether a box is open. A run of four or more colons is a stray wherever it stands. Outside a box, three colons open one, whatever follows them. Inside a box, three colons alone close it. Three colons followed by a name or braces would open a box inside the open one, so that line is a stray too. `nextBox`, which the chapter "Wefts" defines beside `boxOf`, then keeps the state's flag: an opening line sets it, a closing line clears it, and a stray leaves it as it was.

=>

const boxFenceProbe = Option.getOrThrow(getProbe(BoxFenceTokenSchema))

const boxed = (weft: LoomWeft, state: ClassifierState): LoomWeft =>
  Option.isSome(state.quote)
    ? weft
    : Match.value(weft).pipe(
        Match.when({ type: 'PreambleWeft' }, (line) => withBox(line, state.inBox)),
        Match.when({ type: 'ProseWeft' }, (line) => withBox(line, state.inBox)),
        Match.orElse(() => weft),
      )

const withBox = <Line extends PreambleWeft | ProseWeft>(line: Line, inBox: boolean): Line =>
  Option.match(boxLineOf(line, inBox), {
    onNone: () => line,
    onSome: (box) => ({ ...line, box }),
  })

const boxLineOf = (line: PreambleWeft | ProseWeft, inBox: boolean): Option.Option<BoxToken> =>
  Option.map(Option.fromNullishOr(boxFenceProbe.exec(line.source)), (match) => {
    const content = line.source.replace(/\r?\n$/, '')
    const colons = match[0].trimStart()
    const column = match[0].length - colons.length
    const at = (from: number, to: number): Position =>
      span(
        line.position.start.line,
        line.position.start.offset + from,
        line.position.start.offset + to,
      )
    return BoxTokenSchema.make({
      position: at(column, content.length),
      source: content.slice(column),
      health: incompleteHealth,
      side: sideOf(colons.length, inBox, content.slice(match[0].length).trim() === ''),
      fence: BoxFenceTokenSchema.make({
        position: at(column, match[0].length),
        source: colons,
        health: okHealth,
      }),
    })
  })

const sideOf = (colons: number, inBox: boolean, bare: boolean): BoxToken['side'] =>
  Match.value({ three: colons === 3, inBox, bare }).pipe(
    Match.when({ three: false }, () => 'stray' as const),
    Match.when({ inBox: false }, () => 'open' as const),
    Match.when({ bare: true }, () => 'close' as const),
    Match.orElse(() => 'stray' as const),
  )

## Building the wefts

A constructor emits the partial weft for each kind, carrying `incompleteHealth` for the tokeniser to settle. `makeHeadingWeft` fills the `headingStart` marker, and `makeArrowWeft` and `makeTildeWeft` fill the arrow and tilde markers from the probe match. `makeFenceWeft` fills the run of backticks on a fence line, at the column where it starts, and records which side of fenced code the line stands on. Its label, if it has one, is left for the tokeniser.

=>

const makeHeadingWeft = (
  lineText: string,
  line: number,
  range: LineRange,
  m: RegExpMatchArray,
): HeadingWeft =>
  HeadingWeftSchema.make({
    position: linePos(line, range),
    source: lineText,
    health: incompleteHealth,
    headingStart: HeadingStartTokenSchema.make({
      position: span(line, range[0], range[0] + m[0].length),
      source: m[0],
      health: okHealth,
    }),
  })

const makeArrowWeft = (
  lineText: string,
  line: number,
  range: LineRange,
  m: RegExpMatchArray,
): ArrowWeft => {
  const start = range[0] + m[0].length - 2
  const end = range[0] + m[0].length
  return ArrowWeftSchema.make({
    position: linePos(line, range),
    source: lineText,
    health: incompleteHealth,
    arrow: ArrowTokenSchema.make({
      position: span(line, start, end),
      source: m[0].slice(-2),
      health: okHealth,
    }),
    anchors: [],
  })
}

const makeTildeWeft = (
  lineText: string,
  line: number,
  range: LineRange,
  m: RegExpMatchArray,
): TildeWeft => {
  const run = /~+$/.exec(m[0])![0]
  const start = range[0] + m[0].length - run.length
  const end = range[0] + m[0].length
  return TildeWeftSchema.make({
    position: linePos(line, range),
    source: lineText,
    health: incompleteHealth,
    tilde: TildeTokenSchema.make({
      position: span(line, start, end),
      source: run,
      health: okHealth,
    }),
    anchors: [],
  })
}

const makeFenceWeft = (
  lineText: string,
  line: number,
  range: LineRange,
  side: FenceWeft['side'],
): FenceWeft => {
  const fence = Option.getOrElse(openingFence(lineText), () => ({ length: 0, column: 0 }))
  const start = range[0] + fence.column
  return FenceWeftSchema.make({
    position: linePos(line, range),
    source: lineText,
    health: incompleteHealth,
    side,
    fence: CodeFenceTokenSchema.make({
      position: span(line, start, start + fence.length),
      source: lineText.slice(fence.column, fence.column + fence.length),
      health: okHealth,
    }),
    anchors: [],
  })
}

~

A plain line bears no marker, so its constructor takes none. `makePreambleWeft`, `makeCodeWeft`, and `makeProseWeft` build the ordinary lines of the three modes.

=>

const makePreambleWeft = (lineText: string, line: number, range: LineRange): PreambleWeft =>
  PreambleWeftSchema.make({
    position: linePos(line, range),
    source: lineText,
    health: incompleteHealth,
    warps: [],
    anchors: [],
  })

const makeCodeWeft = (lineText: string, line: number, range: LineRange): CodeWeft =>
  CodeWeftSchema.make({
    position: linePos(line, range),
    source: lineText,
    health: incompleteHealth,
    anchors: [],
  })

const makeProseWeft = (lineText: string, line: number, range: LineRange): ProseWeft =>
  ProseWeftSchema.make({
    position: linePos(line, range),
    source: lineText,
    health: incompleteHealth,
    anchors: [],
  })

~

A frontmatter line splits two ways. `makeFrontmatterFence` builds a `---`, already whole, and `makeFrontmatterField` a membership or `key: value` line, left for the tokeniser to fill. `makeTocWeft` builds one line of a contents section the same way — a part heading, a chapter item, or a blank — its parts left for the tokeniser. `makeDividerWeft` builds a divider whole, its dashes and all, since nothing on its line waits for the tokeniser.

=>

const makeFrontmatterFence = (lineText: string, line: number, range: LineRange): FrontmatterWeft =>
  FrontmatterWeftSchema.make({
    position: linePos(line, range),
    source: lineText,
    health: okHealth,
    fence: FrontmatterFenceTokenSchema.make({
      position: span(line, range[0], range[0] + 3),
      source: '---',
      health: okHealth,
    }),
  })

const makeFrontmatterField = (lineText: string, line: number, range: LineRange): FrontmatterWeft =>
  FrontmatterWeftSchema.make({
    position: linePos(line, range),
    source: lineText,
    health: incompleteHealth,
  })

const makeDividerWeft = (lineText: string, line: number, range: LineRange): DividerWeft =>
  DividerWeftSchema.make({
    position: linePos(line, range),
    source: lineText,
    health: okHealth,
    dashes: DividerTokenSchema.make({
      position: span(line, range[0], range[0] + lineText.trimEnd().length),
      source: lineText.trimEnd(),
      health: okHealth,
    }),
  })

const makeTocWeft = (lineText: string, line: number, range: LineRange): TocWeft =>
  TocWeftSchema.make({
    position: linePos(line, range),
    source: lineText,
    health: incompleteHealth,
  })

## What this module draws on

Effect supplies `Stream` for the accumulating scan and `Match` for the dispatch. The `LineRange` type comes from the line scanner, the marker probes and schemas from `LoomTokens`, the weft schemas and `nextBox` from `Weft`, and `incompleteHealth`, `okHealth`, and `Position` from the foundation.

=>

import { Context, Effect, Layer, Match, Option, Stream, pipe } from 'effect'
import type { LineRange } from './LineRanges'
import { incompleteHealth, okHealth, type Position } from '@athrio/loom-ast/LoomNode'
import {
  ArrowTokenSchema,
  BoxFenceTokenSchema,
  type BoxToken,
  BoxTokenSchema,
  CodeFenceTokenSchema,
  DividerTokenSchema,
  closesFence,
  type Fence,
  FrontmatterFenceTokenSchema,
  HeadingStartTokenSchema,
  openingFence,
  TildeTokenSchema,
  getProbe,
} from '@athrio/loom-ast/LoomTokens'
import {
  type ArrowWeft,
  ArrowWeftSchema,
  type CodeWeft,
  CodeWeftSchema,
  type DividerWeft,
  DividerWeftSchema,
  type FenceWeft,
  FenceWeftSchema,
  type FrontmatterWeft,
  FrontmatterWeftSchema,
  type HeadingWeft,
  HeadingWeftSchema,
  type LoomWeft,
  type PreambleWeft,
  PreambleWeftSchema,
  type ProseWeft,
  ProseWeftSchema,
  type TildeWeft,
  TildeWeftSchema,
  type TocWeft,
  TocWeftSchema,
  nextBox,
} from '@athrio/loom-ast/Weft'

